AUX-CONTEXT|<work>/refute-out/run/aux_src_single_sided_lp_AbstractSingleSidedLP_sol_initiateWithdraw.txt
Tracing the resolved path:

  1. __initiateWithdraw exits the pool proportionally and then delegatecalls BaseLPLib.initiateWithdraw with the per-leg
  exitBalances.
  2. Inside that loop, for each non-zero leg the code does IWithdrawRequestManager manager =
  ADDRESS_REGISTRY.getWithdrawRequestManager(address(tokens[i])) and then, unlike hasPendingWithdrawals and
  tokenizeWithdrawRequest, it does not continue on a zero manager — it immediately performs the high-level external call
  manager.initiateWithdraw(...).
  3. For the leg recorded as the ETH sentinel (address(0) per the TOKENS() contract, as the derived
  CurveConvex2Token.TOKENS() just returns the immutable token addresses), the registry lookup is keyed on the sentinel.
  Native ETH is not a yield token with a withdraw queue, so the registry returns address(0); a Solidity high-level
  interface call to an address with no code fails the extcodesize check and reverts. The entire initiateWithdraw
  transaction reverts atomically — no request is booked, no shares are escrowed, and convertToAssets never reaches the
  escrow branch. The claimed "no-op approve then phantom request" state is unreachable.
  4. The only way the call does not revert at step 3 is if governance has explicitly registered a withdraw-request
  manager under the ETH sentinel key. That registration is privileged configuration, and such a manager would have to be
  written to source its value from msg.value (or from a transferFrom that is impossible on address(0)) — either way it
  reverts on a zero-value, zero-approval invocation rather than silently minting an unbacked request. The claim asserts
  the manager "books a withdraw request denominated in exitBalances[i] units of its own ERC-20 yield token" without
  pointing to any manager implementation that credits an amount it never received; that behaviour is present in neither
  the file under review nor the derived contract.
  5. Independently, the escrow pricing path BaseLPLib.getWithdrawRequestValue iterates all tokens and
  require(hasRequest) for each leg, so a half-populated request set cannot be priced either — it would revert rather
  than double-count.

  The exploit therefore depends on (i) a registry entry that does not exist for a native-ETH sentinel and, if it did,
  would be privileged configuration, and (ii) an unshown manager implementation that credits unreceived value. Neither
  is established by the code.

  CONSTRAINT|class=C22|A claim that a value-bearing leg is booked without backing must identify, in code, both the
  registry/lookup path that actually resolves to a live counterparty for that leg and the counterparty logic that
  credits an amount it never received — a lookup that resolves to a codeless address reverts the whole call, and a
  lookup that resolves only via operator registration is privileged configuration.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/single-sided-lp/AbstractSingleSidedLP.sol:initiateWithdraw|class=C22|The ETH-sentinel leg's
  registry lookup yields no withdraw-request manager, so the unguarded high-level call into a codeless address reverts
  the entire initiateWithdraw atomically — no phantom request is ever recorded — and any manager registered under the
  sentinel would be privileged configuration whose credit-without-receipt behaviour appears in neither file.
